<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Dream Jobs – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> What Skills Do I Need for a Future Career?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose a detailed personal narrative. Interactive: Write collaborative stories or articles.
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose a detailed essay explaining why they chose a dream job, developing ideas across an introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion (e.g., "I chose engineering because I enjoy solving problems. I also like working on projects that require creativity. In the future, I hope to contribute to sustainable building projects.").
            Can write a story with peers about a future job (e.g., "In the future, I will be working in technology.").
            Can collaboratively write a description of skills needed for future careers (e.g., "New jobs will require creativity and problem-solving.").
            </p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various advanced technologies (e.g., AI robots, remote work setups, sustainable energy projects). Students are asked to write down three jobs they think *will exist* or *will disappear* in the next 20 years, and one skill that *will be* in high demand. They write their answers on mini-whiteboards or slips of paper.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to describe a future job using future simple and continuous. For example: "I believe many jobs related to <i>AI (artificial intelligence)</i> *will emerge*. People *will be working* remotely more often. Adaptability *will be* a crucial skill." The teacher highlights the grammar structures and pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "automation" and "adaptability."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a job *will emerge*, does it exist now or will it start in the future?" (Answer: Future)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'people *will be working* remotely,' am I talking about a short action or an ongoing situation in the future?" (Answer: Ongoing situation)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short article excerpt (genre: narrative/informative, register: semi-formal) titled "Shaping Tomorrow: The Evolution of Dream Careers." The text describes future job *market* *trends*, *automation*, *remote work*, and the *innovation* required for *advancement* in various *industries*, including examples of individuals pursuing their *dream jobs* in these *evolving* *fields*. It explicitly uses future simple, future continuous, and modals of possibility.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret the main ideas and specific details in written texts about future careers and required skills, identifying relevant information for writing. (Adapted from MEDUCA's interpretation of CEFR B1.1 for reading comprehension in preparation for writing)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the article individually and complete the following in writing:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Write down the main idea of the article in one sentence. What is the article *predicting* about the *future* of *employment*?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match three future *skills* (e.g., *adaptability*, creativity, *technological* literacy) mentioned in the text to the *industries* where they *will be* most *in-demand*.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Read specific statements about *automation* and *remote work* from the text and mark them True or False, correcting the false ones by writing the accurate information.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing accurate sentences and short paragraphs using future simple (for predictions), future continuous (for ongoing future actions/goals), and modals of possibility (may, might, could). Focus on vocabulary related to *jobs*, *skills*, *future*, *demand*, *advancement*, *technology*, *innovation*, *adaptability*, *evolve*, *require*, *develop*, *learn*, *prepare*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences related to future careers using the correct form of the verb in parentheses (future simple/continuous) and a suitable modal.
                            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In five years, I (work) ______ in the technology *field*."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My dream job (require) ______ a lot of creativity; it (may/might/could) ______ also *demand* strong problem-solving *skills*."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"New *opportunities* (evolve) ______ as *automation* (change) ______ the *workforce*."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Paragraph Building:</b> Students are given sentence starters and a list of target vocabulary (e.g., *career*, *advancement*, *adaptability*, *innovation*, *foresee*, *develop*) to write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) about a *future* *job* they are interested in.
                            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example prompt: "My ideal future job is... In this *career*, I *will be working* on... This role *will require*... I *may need* to *acquire* new *skills* like..."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Writing & Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students collaboratively write a short description (5-6 sentences) of a *future* *job* that *will demand* a unique combination of *skills*. They must use at least two instances of future simple, two of future continuous, and one modal of possibility. After writing, pairs exchange their descriptions with another pair and provide feedback on grammar accuracy, vocabulary usage, and clarity. The teacher circulates, offering guidance on pronunciation of target words like "/dʒ/ change" and "/ʒ/ vision" if students read sentences aloud.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual detailed personal narrative essay (3 paragraphs).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connecting to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students will compose a detailed essay about their "Dream Job," focusing on "What Skills Do I Need for a Future Career?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students will write a three-paragraph essay (introduction, body, conclusion) explaining their chosen dream job.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Paragraph 1 (Introduction):</b> Introduce the dream job and briefly state why it was chosen.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Paragraph 2 (Body):</b> Describe the *skills* required for this job, how *technology* *will impact* it, and what they *will be doing* in this *career* in ten years. They must integrate at least three specific target vocabulary words (e.g., *innovation*, *adaptability*, *advancement*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Paragraph 3 (Conclusion):</b> Summarize their aspirations and reflect on how they *will prepare* for this *future* *employment* by *acquiring* necessary *skills* through *education* or *learning*.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are explicitly instructed to use future simple (for predictions), future continuous (for long-term goals/ongoing actions), and modals for possibilities (e.g., "My job *may require* constant *learning* of new technologies."). They should aim to *explain personal career goals* and implicitly include advice on *skills needed for evolving job markets* within their narrative.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and a simple rubric for the written essay.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their detailed personal narrative essay from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric Snapshot):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content & Idea Development (50%):</b> Clear introduction, well-developed body paragraphs explaining the dream job, required *skills*, *technological impact*, and future activities. Logical conclusion. (Learning Outcome met: Can compose a detailed essay...)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar & Vocabulary (30%):</b> Accurate use of future simple, future continuous, and modals of possibility. Appropriate use of at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., *demand*, *advancement*, *automation*, *adaptability*, *innovation*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Organization & Cohesion (20%):</b> Essay follows a clear three-paragraph structure. Sentences are well-connected, leading to clear communication.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short written reflection:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new vocabulary word I used successfully in my essay is __________."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found using the (future simple/future continuous/modals) to be (easy/challenging) because __________."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Review, self-assessment, and future planning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about your dream job and future *skills* did you find most interesting or challenging today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *will* improving your writing *skills* help you in your *future* *career* or academic endeavors?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>What worked well?</b> The collaborative writing in Stage 3 engaged students and provided immediate peer feedback. The structure for the essay helped guide students in organizing their thoughts and integrating target grammar/vocabulary. Many students accurately used future simple and target nouns like "technology" and "adaptability."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>What adjustments/scaffolds are needed for next time?</b> Some students struggled with the future continuous and complex sentence structures for modals of possibility. More focused practice or sentence frames for these specific structures might be beneficial. Perhaps providing a model essay for structure without giving away too much content.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration):</b> In the next lesson, students *will be* working on consolidating their ideas and knowledge from the entire unit to complete the 21st Century Skills Project. They *will mediate* information from their essays and discussions into a presentation or digital project, integrating the written skills practiced today with speaking and digital literacy.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>